WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.373
- Good morning Believe nation.

00:00:01.373 --> 00:00:04.193
Today's message is hustle.

00:00:04.193 --> 00:00:05.768
Over to you, Timothy Sykes.

00:00:05.768 --> 00:00:08.218
(rooster crowing)

00:00:08.218 --> 00:00:10.951
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:10.951 --> 00:00:13.267
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:00:13.267 --> 00:00:16.767
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:17.987 --> 00:00:21.092
- The key to being
successful is to work harder

00:00:21.092 --> 00:00:24.212
than other people, 'cause
everybody wants success.

00:00:24.212 --> 00:00:25.382
Everybody wants a Lamborghini.

00:00:25.382 --> 00:00:30.009
Everybody wants freedom, but
what are you willing to do

00:00:30.009 --> 00:00:31.809
to get there?

00:00:31.809 --> 00:00:33.390
That's my question to you.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.550
We're here at the most
prestigious university

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.909
in America, Harvard.

00:00:37.909 --> 00:00:39.054
Fantastic, I'm very honored.

00:00:39.054 --> 00:00:41.590
Thank you for having me here.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:43.758
The people who have gotten here
have worked their butts off

00:00:43.758 --> 00:00:47.150
to get here, but that's
just the beginning.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:49.633
Whether you're in college or
you're going to go to college,

00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:53.044
or you're out of college,
this is a lifelong journey

00:00:53.044 --> 00:00:54.906
where you have to continually work hard,

00:00:54.906 --> 00:00:58.783
work harder than other
people to get ahead.

00:00:58.783 --> 00:01:01.783
A lot of people give
up at different times.

00:01:01.783 --> 00:01:04.220
I'm no longer at that stage.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:07.647
When I had my big loss,
I basically gave up.

00:01:07.647 --> 00:01:09.868
For a year I was in the
gutter, but I came back.

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:10.967
And I said, "You know what?

00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:14.035
"This was a mistake that I made investing.

00:01:14.035 --> 00:01:15.087
"It wasn't the patterns.

00:01:15.087 --> 00:01:16.816
"I didn't go against
what I learned to make

00:01:16.816 --> 00:01:19.696
"all my millions before this loss.

00:01:19.696 --> 00:01:23.934
"I invested," and I didn't
do my full due diligence,

00:01:23.934 --> 00:01:25.832
and that was my mistake.

00:01:25.832 --> 00:01:27.173
I eventually got back into trading.

00:01:27.173 --> 00:01:29.791
I saw more patterns, and
I became the number one

00:01:29.791 --> 00:01:34.289
lane trader at a 50,000 on
Covestor in 2007 to 2011.

00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:35.874
- Every time I think about this message,

00:01:35.874 --> 00:01:38.937
I think about Jerry Seinfeld,
and one of my favorite

00:01:38.937 --> 00:01:41.966
bits of advice from Jerry
Seinfeld is you need to find

00:01:41.966 --> 00:01:45.636
the thing that most
people consider torture.

00:01:45.636 --> 00:01:48.291
It's the torture that you can endure,

00:01:48.291 --> 00:01:50.037
where, when people look at your schedule,

00:01:50.037 --> 00:01:52.268
they would see it as torturous.

00:01:52.268 --> 00:01:53.187
Look at what I do.

00:01:53.187 --> 00:01:54.599
Look what I do on Youtube.

00:01:54.599 --> 00:01:59.035
So many people write in,
they Tweet me, they email me

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:01.749
asking, "How do you do so many videos?

00:02:01.749 --> 00:02:05.749
"I'm having a hard time
doing one video a week".

00:02:08.115 --> 00:02:09.198
It's torture.

00:02:10.478 --> 00:02:13.824
If you were to look up my
schedule, it would be torture.

00:02:13.824 --> 00:02:16.976
Making three videos at least a day,

00:02:16.976 --> 00:02:20.934
going to the Youtube channel
on top of responding to

00:02:20.934 --> 00:02:22.142
all the comments that come in.

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:25.914
We get hundreds of comments
every day that we respond to,

00:02:25.914 --> 00:02:28.918
on top of keeping track of
all the requests, right,

00:02:28.918 --> 00:02:33.199
from the top 10 series we
have over 2,500 requests,

00:02:33.199 --> 00:02:34.778
and we keep track of it.

00:02:34.778 --> 00:02:36.746
We keep note and see how many people voted

00:02:36.746 --> 00:02:39.122
for different people, and then
we start making those videos,

00:02:39.122 --> 00:02:41.517
on top of coming up with new series ideas

00:02:41.517 --> 00:02:42.633
for what we can test out.

00:02:42.633 --> 00:02:45.401
It'd be so easy just to
keep with the same formula,

00:02:45.401 --> 00:02:46.762
but it's not enough.

00:02:46.762 --> 00:02:47.662
It's got to get better.

00:02:47.662 --> 00:02:48.495
We got to grow.

00:02:48.495 --> 00:02:49.345
We got to improve.

00:02:49.345 --> 00:02:50.414
It's torture.

00:02:50.414 --> 00:02:52.222
You would look at our schedule and say,

00:02:52.222 --> 00:02:55.405
"There's no way I want
to do that much work,"

00:02:55.405 --> 00:02:57.231
but it's why I've gotten better.

00:02:57.231 --> 00:03:00.769
It's why I went from the worst
summit agency to the best,

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:03.466
because, while everybody
else was having trouble

00:03:03.466 --> 00:03:06.813
doing one video a week, I was
doing multiple videos a day.

00:03:06.813 --> 00:03:10.205
It's that hustle, and it's
not to say that I'm great.

00:03:10.205 --> 00:03:12.084
Anybody can accomplish it.

00:03:12.084 --> 00:03:14.182
You just have to find
the thing that you can do

00:03:14.182 --> 00:03:16.010
that would look like
torture to other people,

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.042
so, as an example, I had
Neil Pasricha on my channel.

00:03:19.042 --> 00:03:22.444
We did his top 10 together, and afterwards

00:03:22.444 --> 00:03:24.370
we were talking about his book.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.203
He has a book.

00:03:25.203 --> 00:03:28.040
It sold over a million copies,
and he was giving me advice

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.399
on how to sell a book, and
one of the bits of advice

00:03:30.399 --> 00:03:34.562
that he gave me was he would
go to all the book stores

00:03:34.562 --> 00:03:37.522
around in the general area,
and in Toronto we've got

00:03:37.522 --> 00:03:39.872
a lot of different book stores,
and find out if they had

00:03:39.872 --> 00:03:42.301
at least one copy of the book in store,

00:03:42.301 --> 00:03:46.595
and he would drive there, and
he would talk to the manager,

00:03:46.595 --> 00:03:49.508
and he would sign the book,
and he would have stickers

00:03:49.508 --> 00:03:54.432
that said signed by author, and
he would chat up the manager

00:03:54.432 --> 00:03:57.508
and talk about why he made
this book, and they would,

00:03:57.508 --> 00:04:00.801
because of that conversation,
profile a little bit more

00:04:00.801 --> 00:04:02.556
at the store, and, when
people came in and asked them

00:04:02.556 --> 00:04:04.605
for a book, they would
give that recommendation,

00:04:04.605 --> 00:04:06.324
and so that's how he would spend his days,

00:04:06.324 --> 00:04:07.778
driving to different
book stores, chatting up

00:04:07.778 --> 00:04:09.781
the different managers, signing the books,

00:04:09.781 --> 00:04:12.948
putting the stickers on,
and I look at that work.

00:04:12.948 --> 00:04:16.782
I think that is torture. (laughs)

00:04:16.782 --> 00:04:18.295
I couldn't do it.

00:04:18.295 --> 00:04:22.974
I would hate to do that,
to drive two hours to go

00:04:22.974 --> 00:04:26.529
to a book store that had
two copies of my book,

00:04:26.529 --> 00:04:29.570
to sign those books,
and chat up the manager

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:33.401
to get him or her to know me,
so they'll recommend the book

00:04:33.401 --> 00:04:35.940
and put it higher up, and
do that day in, day out.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:37.321
I couldn't do it.

00:04:37.321 --> 00:04:38.601
It's torture,

00:04:38.601 --> 00:04:40.956
but that's what he did,
that's what he does,

00:04:40.956 --> 00:04:43.793
and that's how he's grown
and created a business

00:04:43.793 --> 00:04:45.952
around his book and his speaking now,

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.587
and he's gone all in on
that, and it's amazing.

00:04:48.587 --> 00:04:50.226
You need to find the same thing for you.

00:04:50.226 --> 00:04:53.609
You need to find the thing you
can dedicate so much time to,

00:04:53.609 --> 00:04:57.542
that you can hustle so hard at
that's it's, one, enjoyable.

00:04:57.542 --> 00:04:59.673
As much as it may seem torturous for you

00:04:59.673 --> 00:05:01.958
to jump into my Youtube
schedule, I like it.

00:05:01.958 --> 00:05:03.495
I enjoy the work I'm doing.

00:05:03.495 --> 00:05:04.820
I look forward to video day.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:05.936
I look forward to looking at the comments.

00:05:05.936 --> 00:05:07.420
I look forward to seeing what new topics

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:08.694
we can come up with.

00:05:08.694 --> 00:05:11.129
I like it, and that's how we get better,

00:05:11.129 --> 00:05:15.046
so what is the thing that
you can do constantly

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.127
and still get fueled by
and improve and get better

00:05:20.127 --> 00:05:22.672
and create more value that
other people would look at

00:05:22.672 --> 00:05:24.922
and say, "That is torture"?

00:05:26.072 --> 00:05:27.128
That's the question of the day.

00:05:27.128 --> 00:05:30.346
Question of the day: what
is a thing that you do,

00:05:30.346 --> 00:05:34.247
that you hustle so hard at,
that nobody's going to touch you

00:05:34.247 --> 00:05:36.507
'cause it's torture for everybody else?

00:05:36.507 --> 00:05:38.254
Leave it in the comments below.

00:05:38.254 --> 00:05:39.764
Then we'll join in discussion.

00:05:39.764 --> 00:05:41.753
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Ken Ritchie.

00:05:41.753 --> 00:05:44.406
Ken, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:05:44.406 --> 00:05:45.239
Your One Word.

00:05:45.239 --> 00:05:47.362
It really, really,
really means a lot to me,

00:05:47.362 --> 00:05:49.053
and I hope you're enjoying reading it.

00:05:49.053 --> 00:05:50.116
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:51.690
I believe in you.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:53.860
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:55.928
and whatever your one word is,

00:05:55.928 --> 00:05:57.170
and I'll see you again tomorrow morning

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:59.666
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:05:59.666 --> 00:06:02.393
♫ I wake up every morning

00:06:02.393 --> 00:06:05.839
♫ Entspresso keep me going

00:06:05.839 --> 00:06:07.619
Hey, Believe nation, for
those of you who want

00:06:07.619 --> 00:06:09.970
a little extra motivation on hustling,

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.809
I've included some bonus clips
that I hope you'll enjoy.

00:06:12.809 --> 00:06:15.569
- People can look at me, and
it almost pisses them off.

00:06:15.569 --> 00:06:17.865
They'll tell us, "That guy gets results?

00:06:17.865 --> 00:06:20.147
"I'm pissed," right, and
kind of piss them off,

00:06:20.147 --> 00:06:22.720
and then they got to deny
it to themselves and stuff.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.061
Yeah, they can even see videos.

00:06:24.061 --> 00:06:27.118
They'll go and say, "That's
a actress. It can't be.

00:06:27.118 --> 00:06:29.554
"He's uglier than me and he's got a girl?

00:06:29.554 --> 00:06:30.602
"Actress," right?

00:06:30.602 --> 00:06:31.780
No, dude.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:33.219
You just didn't go out.

00:06:33.219 --> 00:06:34.802
I did, you didn't.

00:06:34.802 --> 00:06:36.720
I've actually thought sometimes
of creating a video called

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.815
What I Did While You Didn't Do It or

00:06:39.815 --> 00:06:41.176
What I Was Doing While You Were, no, no.

00:06:41.176 --> 00:06:42.890
I was thinking of calling
it What I Was Doing

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:44.934
While You Were Doing What You Were Doing,

00:06:44.934 --> 00:06:46.483
and I was going to enact the whole thing,

00:06:46.483 --> 00:06:49.794
so I'd show what you
were doing: watching TV.

00:06:49.794 --> 00:06:52.593
Like this, what I was doing:
me just hustling through

00:06:52.593 --> 00:06:56.932
my life, going through my boring,
little, menial, repetitive

00:06:56.932 --> 00:07:00.160
tasks that I do every (audio cuts out) day

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.660
that improve me 0.000001%

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.186
and that add up over time.

00:07:07.186 --> 00:07:08.672
I really think I should
make a video of myself

00:07:08.672 --> 00:07:10.578
just going through my day
and just show me doing it

00:07:10.578 --> 00:07:13.341
every (audio cuts out)
day so people can see

00:07:13.341 --> 00:07:16.642
the menial, slow, boring
nature of progress,

00:07:16.642 --> 00:07:19.361
because, for every
public victory you have,

00:07:19.361 --> 00:07:21.214
you have a private victory.

00:07:21.214 --> 00:07:23.562
For every kick (audio cuts
out) video I put up online,

00:07:23.562 --> 00:07:26.110
there's those hundreds of
hours that I spent at home

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:29.780
looking at my video footage,
looking at other speakers,

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:32.362
comparing it, contrasting
it, and learning how

00:07:32.362 --> 00:07:33.649
to be a better speaker.

00:07:33.649 --> 00:07:36.466
For every hot seat video you
see of me where I do some epic

00:07:36.466 --> 00:07:39.039
(audio cuts out) that blows
your mind, there's all that time

00:07:39.039 --> 00:07:41.949
that I spent going out
doing menial, little

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:44.156
bull (audio cuts out) approaches
again and again and again

00:07:44.156 --> 00:07:45.873
and then going home and
looking at what I did

00:07:45.873 --> 00:07:48.422
and how I could improve it, and on and on.

00:07:48.422 --> 00:07:51.150
For every kick (audio cuts
out) freaking out boot camp

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:52.921
student that I have
that loved the program,

00:07:52.921 --> 00:07:55.179
there's those (audio cuts
out) 50 weekends a year

00:07:55.179 --> 00:07:57.240
that I went out and taught,
learned every little nuance

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.778
of the (audio cuts out) thing
again and again and again

00:07:59.778 --> 00:08:00.861
for a decade.

00:08:01.772 --> 00:08:03.277
That's kind of like what success is like,

00:08:03.277 --> 00:08:07.892
I hope that I provide an
example of a guy who is

00:08:07.892 --> 00:08:12.059
untalented, funny-looking, and
shouldn't be able to do it,

00:08:13.269 --> 00:08:16.704
and is stunting on all your
(audio cuts out) faces,

00:08:16.704 --> 00:08:19.713
(audience laughs)

00:08:19.713 --> 00:08:24.138
and that you can see
that and say, "Oh, shit,

00:08:24.138 --> 00:08:27.094
"if that little (audio cuts
out) can do it, I can do it".

00:08:27.094 --> 00:08:28.658
- [Interviewer] How early in life

00:08:28.658 --> 00:08:30.845
did your relentless ambition begin?

00:08:30.845 --> 00:08:32.409
Did this happen when you were very young?

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:34.048
You alluded to the fact
that you sold papers

00:08:34.048 --> 00:08:36.048
at age six or something.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.320
- I don't know.

00:08:39.186 --> 00:08:42.026
I don't really feel
like I'm that ambitious.

00:08:42.026 --> 00:08:43.375
- [Interviewer] Come on,
I'd hate to follow you

00:08:43.375 --> 00:08:45.100
around for a week.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:45.933
I'd be in a tomb somewhere.

00:08:45.933 --> 00:08:46.840
- Yeah, well, I just work hard,
but I know a lot of people

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:47.673
that work hard.

00:08:47.673 --> 00:08:51.102
My father worked hard and
set an example for me,

00:08:51.102 --> 00:08:53.317
and I know a lot of people that work hard.

00:08:53.317 --> 00:08:54.217
- [Interviewer] What does
that mean, working hard?

00:08:54.217 --> 00:08:55.150
What is your day like?

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:58.067
- Work hard is hustle all day long.

00:08:58.956 --> 00:09:01.744
Talk about hustlers, Pete
Rose on the baseball field,

00:09:01.744 --> 00:09:03.497
I try and do that all day long,

00:09:03.497 --> 00:09:06.455
and that's what I think we
need to do here in America.

00:09:06.455 --> 00:09:09.349
The people working in the
American automobile industry,

00:09:09.349 --> 00:09:11.072
we got to out-hustle the Japanese.

00:09:11.072 --> 00:09:13.565
We got to get that old roll up our sleeves

00:09:13.565 --> 00:09:14.735
and get back to work.

00:09:14.735 --> 00:09:16.291
Then we'll get this
country out of trouble.

00:09:16.291 --> 00:09:18.154
We can do anything
better than anybody else

00:09:18.154 --> 00:09:18.987
in the world, I think.

00:09:18.987 --> 00:09:19.820
- [Interviewer] You bet your
life, there's no person--

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:23.526
- But we got to reawake that spirit.

00:09:23.526 --> 00:09:25.353
That's what I'm trying to
do with the Atlanta Braves

00:09:25.353 --> 00:09:28.286
is get everybody hustling,
'cause it sets a good example

00:09:28.286 --> 00:09:30.815
for the people that are
watching the program.

00:09:30.815 --> 00:09:31.648
- [Interviewer] You want another--

00:09:31.648 --> 00:09:33.481
- Fear is the disease.

00:09:34.431 --> 00:09:38.381
Hustle is the antidote, so, in my day,

00:09:38.381 --> 00:09:40.538
I already sound like an old school dude.

00:09:40.538 --> 00:09:43.562
In my day it was just tough, man.

00:09:43.562 --> 00:09:48.089
You start a company in
2001, good luck, right?

00:09:48.089 --> 00:09:49.607
You can't count on funding.

00:09:49.607 --> 00:09:51.094
You can't count on sales.

00:09:51.094 --> 00:09:54.561
You can't count on anything
but just crazy hustle

00:09:54.561 --> 00:09:58.728
and just grit your teeth,
claw your way to success.

00:10:00.226 --> 00:10:02.838
There was just no easy way to do it.

00:10:02.838 --> 00:10:05.660
What I find is a lot of
the entrepreneurs today

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:09.081
are much smarter than
we were back in the day.

00:10:09.081 --> 00:10:11.043
They're like way smarter.

00:10:11.043 --> 00:10:11.876
They got blogs.

00:10:11.876 --> 00:10:14.171
They know what VCs are doing.

00:10:14.171 --> 00:10:16.697
They know everything that's
going on in the space.

00:10:16.697 --> 00:10:20.186
There's a corpus of knowledge
now that makes entrepreneurs

00:10:20.186 --> 00:10:25.009
today much smarter, but
there's liquidity in funding,

00:10:25.009 --> 00:10:26.323
especially on the angel side.

00:10:26.323 --> 00:10:28.374
It used to be much harder
to get an angel deal done

00:10:28.374 --> 00:10:29.589
than a series A deal.

00:10:29.589 --> 00:10:34.409
Now, angel deals are a piece
of cake, but what happens is

00:10:34.409 --> 00:10:38.576
I see a number of entrepreneurs
who don't have that hustle,

00:10:40.124 --> 00:10:43.003
and they're afraid, and
they act out of that fear,

00:10:43.003 --> 00:10:46.215
and, when you act out of that
fear, you make big mistakes.

00:10:46.215 --> 00:10:48.941
An example might be doing
a preempted deal with a VC

00:10:48.941 --> 00:10:50.884
thinking they're your friend.

00:10:50.884 --> 00:10:55.139
An example might be not being
hardcore in keeping your job.

00:10:55.139 --> 00:10:58.722
An example might be,
a lot of times it gets

00:10:59.778 --> 00:11:00.915
exposed through deals.

00:11:00.915 --> 00:11:04.534
You see that fear being
exposed through the deal cycle,

00:11:04.534 --> 00:11:08.755
and, if you're an entrepreneur
and you find yourself

00:11:08.755 --> 00:11:11.672
afraid of something, sack up, okay,

00:11:14.522 --> 00:11:16.105
and start hustling.

